Detect stateless resets behind another connection's ID; allow not sending them.

Applies to picoquic 70b5fd5aafdd2d6c349ff783202ece9d778bcaf2, after 0001 to
0008.

- A packet associated with a connection by its leading bytes but not
  decryptable was compared with that connection's token only, so a reset for
  connection B whose leading bytes happen to name connection A was missed
  (RFC 9000 Section 10.3.1). The comparison in
  picoquic_parse_header_and_decrypt now falls through to the pairs
  registered for the remote address, and the connection it names is the one
  reset. Its direct comparison uses picoquic_constant_time_memcmp.
- picoquic_set_stateless_reset_sending(quic, 0) makes a context never answer
  an unassociated packet with a stateless reset (Section 10.3, a MAY) while
  it keeps receiving and detecting them: an application running several
  contexts on one port leaves the answer to the context that owns the
  connection. picoquic_set_default_stateless_reset_min_interval remains a
  rate limit and cannot disable sending.

The registered-pair refresh and the table comparison come from 0004.
Tests (picoquic_ct): stateless_reset_after_rebinding,
stateless_reset_other_cnx_prefix and stateless_reset_sending_disabled, each
through picoquic_incoming_packet. In LibMoQ:
adapters/picoquic/tests/test_pq_stateless_reset_contract.c.

